@startuml browser-integration
!theme plain
title Browser Integration & IPC — Sequence and Class Comparison
caption How browser extensions communicate with XDM across implementations

skinparam nodesep 20
skinparam ranksep 20
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFFF0
  BorderColor #999999
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
' SHARED BROWSER EXTENSION (all implementations use this)
' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "Browser Extension (Chrome/Firefox/Edge)" as ext #FFF2CC {
  class "messaging.js" as ext_msg {
    +sendWithNativeMessaging(data)
    +sendViaHttp(data)
    --
    Native messaging preferred;
    falls back to HTTP localhost
  }
  class "bg2.js / bg3.js" as ext_bg {
    +onBeforeRequest(details)
    +onHeadersReceived(details)
    +sendImageToXDM(info)
    +detectVideo(url, headers)
  }
  class "util.js" as ext_util {
    +addToValueList()
    +isVideoMimeType()
    +isVideoUrl()
  }

  ext_bg --> ext_msg : sends captured data
  ext_bg --> ext_util : uses

  note bottom of ext_bg
    **⚠ Known bugs (see CODE_REVIEW.md):**
    • addToValueList overwrites (missing else)
    • sendImageToXDM missing var (global leak)
    • firefox-old/bg.js log() recursive overflow
    • sendRecUrl off-by-one
    • sendWithNativeMessaging data += coerces object
    • Debug mode hardcoded true in production
  end note
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
' JAVA IPC
' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "Java — xdman.monitoring" #DAE8FC {

  class "BrowserMonitor" as j_mon {
    «Runnable»
    -serverSocket : ServerSocket
    -port : 9614
    --
    +run()
    -acceptConnection()
    -syncSettingsJSON()
  }

  class "MonitoringSession" as j_sess {
    «Runnable»
    -socket : Socket
    --
    +run()
    -parseRequest()
    -processDownload()
    -processVideo()
  }

  class "Request / Response" as j_rr {
    +method : String
    +path : String
    +headers : Map
    +body : String
  }

  together {
    class "M3U8Handler" as j_m3u8
    class "F4mHandler" as j_f4m
    class "FBHandler" as j_fb
    class "VimeoHandler" as j_vim
    class "InstagramHandler" as j_ig
  }

  j_mon --> j_sess : spawns per connection
  j_sess --> j_rr : parses
  j_sess --> j_m3u8 : delegates
  j_sess --> j_f4m
  j_sess --> j_fb
  j_sess --> j_vim
  j_sess --> j_ig

  note bottom of j_mon
    **Java: ServerSocket on :9614**
    One Thread per browser connection.
    Parses HTTP requests manually.
    Site-specific handlers for video detection.
    Syncs config to ~/.xdman/settings.json
    for browser extension to read.
  end note
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
' C# IPC
' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "C# — XDM.Core.BrowserMonitoring" #D5E8D4 {

  class "BrowserMonitor" as c_mon {
    «static class»
    +Run()
    +Stop()
  }

  class "NativeMessagingHostHandler" as c_nmh {
    -pipeName : "XDM_Ipc_Browser_Monitoring_Pipe"
    --
    +BroadcastConfig()
    +SendToAllChannels()
  }
  note right of c_nmh
    **⚠ Named pipe with no ACL**
    Any local process can connect.
    Hardcoded well-known pipe name.
  end note

  class "VideoTracker" as c_vt {
    «IVideoTracker»
    -ytVideoList
    -hlsVideoList
    -dashVideoList
    --
    +AddVideo()
    +ClearVideoList()
    <<events>>
    +MediaAdded
    +MediaUpdated
  }

  class "HttpServer" as c_srv {
    -listener : TcpListener
    --
    +Start()
    +Stop()
    +ProcessRequest()
    <<events>>
    +RequestReceived
  }

  class "IpcHttpMessageProcessor" as c_proc {
    +ProcessMessage(context)
  }

  class "PipeChannel" as c_pipe {
    +Read() : Message
    +Write(message)
  }

  class "NativeMessagingHostApp" as c_nma {
    «separate process»
    -stdin / stdout
    --
    +ReadMessage() : RawBrowserMessage
    +WriteMessage(data)
  }

  c_mon --> c_srv : HTTP channel
  c_mon --> c_nmh : pipe channel
  c_srv --> c_proc : HTTP messages
  c_nmh --> c_pipe : pipe messages
  c_proc --> c_vt : detected videos
  c_nma .right.> c_nmh : connects via pipe

  note bottom of c_mon
    **C#: Dual IPC channels**
    1. HTTP server on localhost (like Java)
    2. Named pipe + NativeMessagingHost process
    Browser → NativeMessagingHost (stdin/stdout)
    → Named pipe → XDM Core
    One Thread per connection (HttpServer).
  end note
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
' KOTLIN IPC
' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "Kotlin — xdm.monitoring" #E1D5E7 {

  class "BrowserMonitor" as k_mon {
    -server : embeddedServer «Ktor»
    -port : 9614
    -_events : MutableSharedFlow<BrowserEvent>
    --
    +start()
    +stop()
    +events : SharedFlow<BrowserEvent>
    -processDownloadRequest()
    -processVideoInfo()
  }

  class "BrowserRequest" as k_req <<@Serializable>> {
    +url : String
    +file : String?
    +headers : Map
    +cookies : String?
    +tabUrl : String?
    +userAgent : String?
  }

  class "VideoItem" as k_vid <<@Serializable>> {
    +id : String
    +text : String
    +info : String
  }

  sealed class "BrowserEvent" as k_evt {
    DownloadRequest(request)
    VideoFound(video)
    LinksReceived(links)
  }

  k_mon --> k_req : deserializes
  k_mon --> k_vid : deserializes
  k_mon ..> k_evt : emits via SharedFlow

  note bottom of k_mon
    **Kotlin: Ktor embedded server on :9614**
    Coroutine-based (no thread per connection).
    Validates localhost origin.
    Emits BrowserEvent via SharedFlow
    for reactive consumption.
    ~200 lines (vs ~900 C#, ~400 Java).
  end note
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
' SEQUENCE: Browser → XDM
' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
note as SEQ
  **IPC Flow (all implementations):**
  1. User clicks download or video detected
  2. Extension bg.js captures URL + headers + cookies
  3. Extension sends via native messaging (preferred) or HTTP POST
  4. XDM receives on localhost:9614 (or named pipe on Windows/C#)
  5. XDM parses request, identifies protocol (HTTP/HLS/DASH)
  6. XDM creates appropriate downloader and starts download
  7. XDM sends config back to extension (monitored file types, etc.)

  **Port:** All implementations use :9614
  **Protocol:** JSON over HTTP POST
end note

